{
  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c4": {
      "anchor": "plain-to-draw-it",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "",
      "excerpt": "",
      "text": "why do we have this for card when it is a two sided thing?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T01:42:31.611Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T01:53:48.481Z",
          "html": "<p>The two sided view's own drawing shows only the side it faces: <code>if it is face down, draw its back, otherwise draw its front</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The card replaces it to draw <b>both</b> sides, back to back, so Solitaire's <code>styles.css</code> can turn the card over in 3D (each side turned half round, its back hidden while it faces away).</li>\n  <li>With one side drawn there's nothing to turn: the face just swaps. Flash cards use the template's drawing, and swap that way.</li>\n  <li>It could go if the template itself drew both sides and carried the turn's styles: then every two sided view turns, flash cards too. That's a change to the built-in template and its CSS, not to the card.</li>\n</ul>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changed. It stays until the template draws both sides itself; say if you want that as a phase.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-11T01:58:34.911Z",
          "text": "change the template to do it the way card does it.  Go ahead and do this now in the background",
          "edited": "2026-11-11T01:59:12.638Z"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T02:36:48.041Z",
          "html": "<p>Being built now, in the background (<code>language-two-sides</code>): the two sided view template draws both sides the way Solitaire's card does, and Solitaire and Solitario drop their own <code>- to draw it</code>. It's on its last checks; I'll answer here again when it's done.</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>No change to this page yet: it reads Solitaire as it was, so the card's own drawing still shows here until the template change is merged.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-11T02:06:14.642Z"
      }
    },
    "c8": {
      "anchor": "plain-card",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "",
      "excerpt": "",
      "text": "let's eliminate all the end colons from the example, you wouldn't need to write it with the bubbling",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T01:47:07.567Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "w:",
      "offset": 25,
      "edited": "2026-11-11T01:56:41.204Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T02:26:48.337Z",
          "html": "<p>Done: no line ends in a colon now, in every file on the page: <code>a \"card\" is a draggable two sided view</code>, <code>- its \"symbol\" is</code>, <code>- to draw it</code>. A colon in the middle of a line stays (<code>- when its moves change: clear the hint</code>).</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Listed in section 6, \"Changes to plain Spell this page assumes\": plain Spell needs its colons today.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-11T02:06:16.836Z"
      }
    },
    "c11": {
      "anchor": "plain-is-a-rank",
      "kind": "section",
      "label": "",
      "excerpt": "",
      "text": "nah, remove this and put it back the way it was.  I think we get this for free now due to language changes?  check",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T01:55:46.513Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "alias is a (rank)",
      "offset": 58,
      "edited": "2026-11-11T01:56:19.468Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T02:26:50.434Z",
          "html": "<p>Checked, with a throwaway parse of a card whose rank and suit have no alias lines: P39's <code>is_listed_value</code> reads <code>it is a queen</code> as <code>rank === \"queen\"</code>, and <code>it is a club</code> / <code>it is a spade</code> as the suit. But <code>it is a 2</code> fails, \"Don't understand\", because a list's NUMBER values don't read that way, and the card's own test says <code>it is a 2 should be no</code>.</p><p>So: the rank's alias is back the way it was, its own line <code>- it \"is a (rank)\"</code> under the rank; the suit's is gone, as Spell reads it for free. Its entry is out of section 6.</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>The rank keeps its alias line until numbers in a list read as values too (a small follow-up to P39, if you want it); the suit drops its.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-11T02:06:22.959Z"
      }
    },
    "c12": {
